<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">German Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kaiserreich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The German Empire was the unified German state proclaimed in 1871 under Prussian leadership after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. It became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading industrial power, acquired colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Pacific and built a large navy, but it was defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and collapsed in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-empire.jpg" alt="German Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Adolf Soetbeer, Otto von Bismarck Vector: B1mbo, Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany_(1867%E2%80%931918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The German Confederation had been created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> on 8 June 1815. After the liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848</a> were crushed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>'s pragmatic Realpolitik took their place. He sought to extend Hohenzollern hegemony across the German states, which meant excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s main German rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1864, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> in 1866 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870–1871 fed a growing pan-German ideal and brought victory for the Kleindeutsche Lösung, the Lesser German solution.</p><p>The empire was founded on 18 January 1871, when the south German states, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and Liechtenstein, joined the North German Confederation. Wilhelm I, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> remained the capital, and Bismarck became chancellor. It was a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> of 25 states, and Prussia held about two thirds of its population and territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bismarck served as chancellor from 1871 to 1890. His tenure began with relatively liberal measures and shifted toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>, marked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulturkampf">Kulturkampf</a> against the Catholic Church. He enacted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> in 1871 and created pioneering social programmes in the 1880s. In 1879 he concluded the Dual Alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1882. In the 1880s the empire entered the colonial race, acquiring territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the Pacific and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> died on 9 March 1888. His son Frederick, who had incurable cancer of the larynx, died on 15 June 1888 after 99 days, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> became emperor. Wishing to rule in his own right, he came into conflict with Bismarck, who resigned in March 1890. Wilhelm II then pursued Weltpolitik, a more aggressive course that abandoned Bismarck's alliance system and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> increasingly isolated.</p><p>After the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> full support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, a blank check in the term of the historian Fritz Fischer. The German plan to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> quickly failed, and the Western Front became a stalemate. In October 1918 the armies were in retreat and the allies had collapsed. The empire fell in the November revolution of 1918 with the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> of 132 billion gold marks and limited the army to 100,000 men. The economic devastation that followed, and the humiliation felt by the German population, are considered leading factors in the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>. Many historians have stressed a German Sonderweg, or special path, as a root of the catastrophe of the 20th century. Large parts of the empire now belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk of March 1918, Russia, by then in civil war, ceded all the Polish lands it had held to the <b>German Empire</b> and Austria-Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Conrad and Frederick at first held the inheritance together and then divided it, probably by about 1218: Conrad took the burgraviate of Nuremberg and founded the Franconian line, which later ruled Brandenburg, Prussia and the <b>German Empire</b>, while Frederick kept the ancestral Swabian lands as Count of Zollern.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg joined the North German Confederation in 1867 and the <b>German Empire</b> in 1871 as a federal state, entering the customs union in 1888 on condition of keeping a free port.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the North German Confederation in 1867 and the <b>German Empire</b> in 1871 as a federal state with its own Senate, entering the German customs union only in 1888, when it was granted a free port.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>After the surrender of January 1871, a conservative National Assembly made Adolphe Thiers head of the executive and signed the Treaty of Frankfurt on 10 May 1871, ceding Alsace and most of Lorraine to the new <b>German Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its federal structure, with sovereign states represented by government envoys, influenced the Bundesrat of the North German Confederation and the <b>German Empire</b>, an element that survives in the Bundesrat of the Federal Republic of Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
